# What this repository publishes: everything needed to run the study, reproduce its numbers and
# check the claims the paper makes by citing the code. Source files are cheap and excluding a cited
# one is the expensive mistake, so all of our own code is tracked. What stays out is data, vendored
# third-party trees we have no licence to redistribute, and anything a tracked script regenerates.

# ── datasets: redistributed by their own sources; checksums in harness/data_manifest.tsv
data/

# ── third-party method repositories: each has its own licence, and six of them ship none at all.
#    models/algorithms/ is ours (the fit/predict wrappers) and is tracked, minus FairGT, which the
#    study excludes (METHOD_EXTENSION_INVENTORY.csv) and which carries 34 MB of adjacency caches.
models/*
!models/algorithms/
models/algorithms/FairGT/
models/algorithms/FairGT-main/
models/algorithms/*FairGT*
models/algorithms/**/adj_files/
models/algorithms/**/data/
models/algorithms/**/dataset/

# ── the raw per-cell store and per-epoch trajectories: 4 GB, and regenerated by the runners.
#    results/ and results_v2/ carry what the paper reports.
harness/results/
harness/tests/

# ── the whole results_v2/ working tree: merged into results/, and its baselines, sensitivity
#    bundles and noise-floor runs are regenerated by build_results.py --baseline
results_v2/

# ── on-screen previews of the paper figures (the PDFs are the deliverable)
figures/src/preview/
harness/results/figures/

# ── the notebook that used to hold the table code. build_tables.py replaces it and is tracked;
#    the notebook is kept locally so the two cannot silently disagree.
results/plot.ipynb

# ── raw artifacts: large and regenerable
*.npz
*.pt
*.pth
*.embedding
*.zip
*.sha256

# ── python / editor / OS
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.venv/
env/
venv/
.DS_Store
*.swp
.vscode/
.idea/
